Abstract

This document defines the internal cognitive memory architecture of the Memory Operating System.

Unlike previous RFCs, which define cognitive concepts, this RFC defines how cognition is organized, activated, consolidated, and evolved.

Memory is no longer treated as persistent storage.

Instead, memory is defined as a dynamic cognitive substrate composed of specialized memory spaces, activation mechanisms, consolidation pipelines, and state transitions.

RFC-0014 introduces the Cognitive Memory Hierarchy (CMH), which becomes the architectural foundation for all future storage, retrieval, planning, execution, and learning subsystems.

1. Purpose

Traditional AI memory systems generally distinguish only:

Short-term memory
Long-term memory

MOS considers this model insufficient.

Intelligent systems require multiple forms of memory that differ in purpose, behavior, persistence, activation, and lifecycle.

This RFC defines those memory spaces and how information flows between them.

2. Cognitive Memory Axiom

MOS defines:

Memory is an active cognitive process rather than a passive storage mechanism.

Memory SHALL participate in:

reasoning,
planning,
learning,
prediction,
evaluation,
adaptation.

Memory SHALL influence cognition continuously.

3. Cognitive Memory Hierarchy (CMH)

MOS organizes cognition into hierarchical memory layers.

                     Attention
                         │
                         ▼
                 Working Memory
                         │
                         ▼
                  Active Context
                         │
        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
        ▼                ▼                ▼
 Semantic Memory   Episodic Memory   World Memory
        │                │                │
        └────────────┬───┴───────────────┘
                     ▼
             Procedural Memory
                     │
                     ▼
            Reflection Memory
                     │
                     ▼
            Predictive Memory
                     │
                     ▼
             Long-Term Archive

Each layer has distinct responsibilities and lifecycle rules.

4. Memory Spaces

A Memory Space is a logical cognitive domain.

A Memory Space defines:

purpose,
activation behavior,
persistence,
ownership,
consolidation rules,
retrieval strategy.

A Memory Space does not define physical storage.

Physical implementation is left to the Storage Specification.

5. Working Memory

Working Memory represents the cognitive state currently under active reasoning.

Characteristics:

Small capacity
High volatility
Frequently updated
Optimized for reasoning latency

Working Memory SHALL contain only information actively required for ongoing cognitive processes.

6. Attention Space

Attention is a subset of Working Memory.

It contains the cognitive elements currently receiving processing priority.

Attention SHALL:

prioritize relevance,
suppress noise,
manage focus,
adapt dynamically during reasoning.

Attention behaves analogously to CPU registers.

7. Active Context

Active Context contains the complete reasoning context currently available to an agent.

It may include:

active goals,
recent observations,
retrieved facts,
relevant procedures,
environmental state,
constraints,
policies.

The Active Context SHALL be assembled dynamically.

8. Semantic Memory

Semantic Memory stores normalized knowledge.

Examples:

entities,
facts,
ontologies,
concepts,
relationships.

Semantic Memory SHALL be:

versioned,
provenance-aware,
confidence-scored,
continuously refined.
9. Episodic Memory

Episodic Memory stores experiences.

Episodes preserve:

chronological order,
observations,
actions,
outcomes,
context.

Episodes SHALL remain immutable after creation.

10. Procedural Memory

Procedural Memory stores reusable knowledge describing how to accomplish objectives.

Examples:

workflows,
skills,
playbooks,
recovery procedures,
deployment guides.

Procedural Memory SHALL support versioning and performance evaluation.

11. Reflection Memory

Reflection Memory stores meta-cognitive knowledge.

Examples:

lessons learned,
failures,
successful strategies,
optimization opportunities,
evaluation summaries.

Reflection Memory drives continuous improvement.

12. World Memory

World Memory represents external reality.

It contains:

environments,
world states,
digital twins,
observations,
simulations,
predictions.

World Memory SHALL distinguish observed reality from inferred or simulated reality.

13. Predictive Memory

Predictive Memory stores hypotheses regarding future states.

Examples:

forecasts,
expected outcomes,
simulations,
scenario analyses,
planning projections.

Predictive Memory SHALL never overwrite observations or facts.

14. Identity Memory

Identity Memory stores cognitive actors.

Examples:

users,
agents,
organizations,
devices,
services.

Identity Memory SHALL preserve trust, authority, and provenance relationships.

15. Governance Memory

Governance Memory stores:

policies,
permissions,
constraints,
approval workflows,
compliance rules.

Governance Memory SHALL participate in every high-impact decision.

16. Resource Memory

Resource Memory records historical and current resource usage.

Examples:

token consumption,
latency,
compute,
financial cost,
energy usage,
storage utilization.

Resource Memory enables optimization and planning.

17. Communication Memory

Communication Memory stores interactions between cognitive actors.

Examples:

delegation,
negotiation,
synchronization,
acknowledgements,
broadcasts,
consensus events.

Communication Memory supports distributed cognition.

18. Memory Activation Model

MOS replaces traditional retrieval with memory activation.

Traditional model:

Query
    │
    ▼
Database Search
    │
    ▼
Results

MOS model:

Stimulus
    │
    ▼
Activation Engine
    │
 ┌──┼─────────────┐
 ▼  ▼             ▼
Facts Episodes Skills
 │   │             │
 └───┴─────┬───────┘
           ▼
     Attention Space
           ▼
      Working Memory
           ▼
        Reasoning

Activation SHALL prioritize semantically related knowledge rather than isolated matches.

19. Activation Propagation

Activation propagates through cognitive relationships.

Activation strength MAY depend on:

semantic similarity,
graph distance,
recency,
confidence,
importance,
usage frequency,
task relevance.

Activation propagation SHALL be bounded to prevent uncontrolled expansion.

20. Memory Consolidation

Information SHALL transition between memory spaces.

Example:

Observation
      │
      ▼
Working Memory
      │
      ▼
Evaluation
      │
      ▼
Reflection
      │
      ▼
Semantic Memory
      │
      ▼
Archive

Consolidation SHALL:

eliminate duplication,
merge equivalent facts,
preserve provenance,
update confidence,
retain history.
21. Memory Reinforcement and Decay

MOS does not delete cognition by default.

Instead, cognitive activation changes over time.

Each Cognitive Element SHOULD maintain:

activation score,
importance score,
reinforcement count,
recall count,
decay rate,
last activation,
consolidation state.

Frequently useful knowledge naturally remains more accessible.

22. Memory Lifecycle

Every Cognitive Element follows a common lifecycle.

