The lifecycle SHALL preserve complete provenance.

23. Memory State Model

Each Memory Space defines its own operational state.

Common states include:

Active
Dormant
Consolidating
Archived
Superseded
Predicted
Simulated
Invalidated

Transitions SHALL be explicit and auditable.

24. Memory Coherence

MOS SHALL maintain coherence across all Memory Spaces.

Conflicting information SHALL trigger:

contradiction detection,
confidence recalculation,
provenance analysis,
optional human review,
reflection.

Coherence is preferred over duplication.

25. Explainability

Any reasoning process SHALL be reconstructable.

MOS SHALL be able to answer:

Which memories were activated?
Why were they activated?
Which memory spaces contributed?
Which elements influenced the decision?
Which memories were ignored?
What confidence did each contribute?
26. Compliance

A MOS implementation complies with RFC-0014 if:

Memory Spaces are represented explicitly.
Working Memory is separate from Long-Term Memory.
Memory activation replaces simple retrieval.
Consolidation preserves provenance.
Decay affects activation rather than deleting knowledge.
Memory transitions are explicit and auditable.
Observed, inferred, simulated, and predicted knowledge remain distinguishable.
Cognitive state can be reconstructed at any point in time.
